  "title": "Sparse rows + `initialSort` — missing cells render gracefully",
  "eyebrow": "Lekcja 3 · DataTable",
  "description": "Verifies two edge cases: rows that omit some column keys (sparse data) and the `initialSort` field that pre-sorts the table on first render.",
  "estimatedMinutes": 4,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "s1-context",
      "title": "Context — missing cells + initial sort",
      "type": "theory",
      "data": {
        "markdown": "Real-world data is often sparse — not every row has every column. The DataTable should render a missing cell as a blank cell, not as `undefined` or as a console error. `compareCells` already sends nulls/undefined to the bottom of the sort regardless of direction.\n\n`initialSort: { key, dir }` lets a course author pre-sort the table — useful when the dataset has a natural primary key (date, score, distance from sun)."
      }
    },
